ur = {"id": "update-rings", "name": "Update Rings",
"description": "Rolling out agent and policy updates safely: ring sequencing, canary metrics, pause criteria, and the rollback discipline.",
"icon": "\U0001f504", "scenarios": []}
ur_lessons = [
L("update-rings", "Rings for updates: the sequencing",
["Agent and policy updates ride rings: RING 0 (IT lab, 5 machines) \u2192 RING 1 (IT + volunteers, 5%) \u2192 RING 2 (broad, 25-50%) \u2192 RING 3 (fleet). Each ring soaks before the next begins \u2014 soak time is where the bad updates reveal themselves.",
"Ring 0 is sacrificial by design: IT's own machines take the update first, with the rollback tested on them. 'Eat your own cooking' is the credibility mechanism \u2014 and IT's pain is contained.",
"Soak durations: 24-48h for agent updates (stability signals need time), shorter for policy-only changes (block-rate signals appear fast). The soak is not idle waiting \u2014 it is active monitoring against the canary metrics.",
"Emergency updates compress rings, never skip them: ring 0 + ring 1 accelerated (hours, not days), with the same metrics and pause criteria. 'Emergency' shortens the soak; it doesn't blind the rollout."],
"RING SEQUENCE\n  ring 0: IT lab (5) \u2014 24h soak, rollback tested here\n  ring 1: IT + volunteers (5%) \u2014 24h soak\n  ring 2: broad (25-50%) \u2014 48h soak\n  ring 3: fleet \u2014 monitored rollout\n  emergency: compressed rings, same metrics",
"Sequence every update through the rings with soak times that fit the risk: long soaks for agent binaries, shorter for policy-only changes. Ring 0 eats its own cooking and tests the rollback \u2014 emergencies compress the rings, never skip them.",
["Skipping ring 0 for 'urgent' updates \u2014 the lab exists for exactly the urgent ones.",
"Soak without monitoring \u2014 the soak's product is the metric reading.",
"Fleet-wide day-one \u2014 the blast radius is the whole company."],
"Sequence an agent update for Harbor Dental's 27 machines: ring populations and soak times?"),
L("update-rings", "Canary metrics: what the rings tell you",
["Three canary metrics per ring: BLOCK RATE delta (policy updates \u2014 spike means regression), AGENT HEALTH delta (agent updates \u2014 drops mean install/compat issues), and SUPPORT TICKET delta (both \u2014 the human sensor).",
"Baselines before the ring: capture each metric's normal for 7 days pre-update. A 'spike' is meaningless without the baseline \u2014 the canary reads DELTAS, not absolutes.",
"Pause criteria, pre-written: block rate +X% \u2192 pause; check-in drop >Y% \u2192 pause; ticket spike \u2192 pause. The criteria are written BEFORE the ring, not improvised during it \u2014 pre-commitment beats panic judgment.",
"Ring 0's real product is the pause-criteria validation: did the metrics catch the (simulated or real) issue? A ring 0 that 'went fine' taught you the metrics work \u2014 that IS the lesson."],
"CANARY CARD (per ring)\n  block rate:   baseline __% -> pause at +__%\n  check-in:     baseline __% -> pause at -__%\n  tickets:      baseline __/day -> pause at __/day\n  decision:     GO / PAUSE / ROLLBACK (pre-committed)",
"Fill a canary card before every update. The pre-written pause criteria are what let you act decisively at 2 AM \u2014 the decision was made calmly, in advance.",
["Canary without baselines \u2014 deltas need a 'normal' to deviate from.",
"Improvised pause criteria mid-incident \u2014 pre-commit, or panic decides.",
"Ignoring the ticket delta \u2014 users are sensors; listen to them."],
"Fill a canary card for a policy-only update: baselines (invented), pause thresholds, decision rules."),
L("update-rings", "Pause and rollback: the discipline",
["PAUSE means: stop the rollout where it is, hold the completed rings, investigate before proceeding. Pause is not failure \u2014 it is the mechanism working. Celebrate pauses; they caught something.",
"ROLLBACK means: revert the completed rings to the previous version/policy. The rollback must be TESTED (in ring 0, before the rollout) \u2014 'we can roll back' untested is a hope, and hopes break at 2 AM.",
"The rollback decision tree: issue isolated to the update + affecting business function \u2192 rollback the affected rings. Issue understood + narrowly fixable \u2192 forward-fix on the paused rings, then resume. Issue unknown \u2192 rollback first, investigate second.",
"Communicate both: 'we paused ring 2 because block rate rose 3x; investigating; rings 0-1 unaffected' \u2014 stakeholders hear control, not chaos. Silence during a pause breeds rumor."],
"PAUSE/ROLLBACK\n  pause:    stop, hold, investigate (mechanism working)\n  rollback: revert completed rings (TESTED in ring 0)\n  decide:   isolated+business-impact -> rollback\n            understood+narrow       -> forward-fix, resume\n            unknown                 -> rollback, then investigate",
"Pre-write the pause criteria on the canary card before the rollout, so the 2 AM decision is already made. Pause is the mechanism working \u2014 investigate, then resume or roll back the tested rollback.",
["Treating pause as failure \u2014 the pause that caught the bad update is the win.",
"Untested rollback \u2014 test it in ring 0 or don't claim it.",
"Silent pauses \u2014 communicate, or rumor communicates for you."],
"Write the pause announcement for: ring 2 paused, block rate 3x baseline, investigating. Two sentences."),
L("update-rings", "Agent updates vs policy updates",
["AGENT updates (new binary version): risk is stability/compat \u2014 can the new agent run on this fleet? Rings are wide, soaks are long (48h+), canary is health + tickets. Rollback = previous agent version.",
"POLICY updates (rule changes): risk is correctness \u2014 does the new policy block the right things? Rings can be faster, soaks shorter (block signals appear in hours), canary is block-rate delta. Rollback = previous policy version (fast).",
"Never bundle them: an agent update and a policy change in the same window means a failure is unattributable \u2014 was it the binary or the rules? Separate the changes, separate the signals.",
"Version pinning: know which agent version each ring runs. 'Latest' is not a version \u2014 pin it, track it, and the rollback target is always explicit."],
"UPDATE TYPES\n  agent:  stability risk | long soak | health canary\n  policy: correctness risk | short soak | block-rate canary\n  rule:   NEVER bundle agent + policy in one window",
"Separate the two update types into different windows: agent updates test stability with long soaks; policy updates test correctness with block-rate canaries. Bundled, their failures are unattributable \u2014 one change per window.",
["Bundled updates \u2014 unattributable failures are unfixable failures.",
"'Latest' as a version \u2014 pin it, or rollback has no target.",
"Same rings for both types \u2014 the risks differ; the sequencing should too."],
"Plan a month with both an agent update and a policy change: how do you sequence them?"),
]
for _l in ur_lessons: new_lessons.append(_l)
