mc_lessons = [
L("macos-agents", "macOS prerequisites: the approval chain",
["macOS agents need OS-level approvals BEFORE they function: System Extension approval (kernel-adjacent monitoring), Full Disk Access (reading what it must protect), and sometimes Accessibility or Network Extension approvals. Without them, the agent installs but is blind.",
"The approvals come via MDM profiles (scalable) or manual user approval in System Settings (tolerable for 5, absurd for 500). MDM enrollment is therefore a macOS deployment prerequisite \u2014 not an optional nice-to-have.",
"Order matters: MDM enroll \u2192 push approval profiles \u2192 verify approvals \u2192 install agent \u2192 verify check-in AND capability (is it actually monitoring?). Installing before approvals is the classic macOS deployment failure.",
"Document the approval set per agent version: OS updates and agent updates can ADD required approvals. The prerequisite list is versioned, not static."],
"MACOS DEPLOY SEQUENCE\n  1. MDM enroll\n  2. push approval profiles (sys ext, FDA, ...)\n  3. verify approvals granted\n  4. install agent\n  5. verify check-in + capability",
"Treat MDM enrollment and profile approvals as the macOS deployment itself: enroll, push profiles, verify grants, then install. An agent without Full Disk Access is present and blind \u2014 verify capability, not just check-in.",
["Installing before approvals \u2014 the agent is present and blind.",
"Manual approvals at scale \u2014 500 users clicking through System Settings is not a plan.",
"Static prerequisite lists \u2014 OS/agent updates add approvals; re-verify."],
"Write Harbor Dental's macOS deploy sequence for their 5 Macs: MDM status, approvals, install, verification."),
L("macos-agents", "What differs on macOS: the operator's map",
["Application layout differs: /Applications is the program directory (the path-rule analog), ~/Applications and ~/Downloads are user-writable (the danger zones). Allowlist thinking translates; the paths change.",
"Execution gates are layered: Gatekeeper (notarization/quarantine) sits UNDER your allowlist. An app can pass Gatekeeper and still be denied by policy \u2014 and an app denied by Gatekeeper never reaches your agent. Know which layer decided.",
"Scripting is native: macOS ships Python, perl, osascript, and zsh \u2014 the living-off-the-land toolkit is built in. Ringfencing's app-to-app leashes matter as much here as on Windows; the binary names differ.",
"Updates come from multiple channels: App Store, vendor updaters, and MDM. Each channel changes hashes differently \u2014 the version-churn lesson applies per channel, not per platform."],
"MACOS MAP\n  programs: /Applications (locked) | ~/Downloads (danger)\n  gates: Gatekeeper UNDER allowlist (know which decided)\n  LOLBins: osascript, python3, perl, zsh (built-in)\n  updates: App Store / vendor / MDM (churn per channel)",
"Learn the macOS layer cake: Gatekeeper under your allowlist, /Applications versus the writable locations, and the built-in scripting toolkit. Triage names the decider first \u2014 the silent console is the clue.",
["Treating macOS as 'Windows with different paths' \u2014 the approval and gate model is genuinely different.",
"Forgetting Gatekeeper in triage \u2014 'blocked' might be Apple, not your policy.",
"User-writable app locations \u2014 ~/Applications is the macOS Downloads-equivalent hole."],
"Map one macOS block: which layer decided (Gatekeeper vs allowlist vs ringfence), and how do you tell?"),
L("macos-agents", "macOS-specific policy tuning",
["Rule types translate with care: certificate rules work via Apple/developer identities; hash rules churn per update channel; path rules must respect the /Applications vs ~/writable split. The strength ladder holds \u2014 the paths change.",
"System apps deserve their own baselines: macOS system binaries update with the OS, and OS upgrades change MANY hashes at once. Plan OS-upgrade windows as policy events: pre-approve the new OS's signed components via Apple identity rules.",
"Privacy approvals interact with policy: an agent denied Full Disk Access can't see what it must control \u2014 which looks like 'the policy isn't working' but is actually 'the prerequisite is missing.' Triage capability before policy.",
"MDM is the policy vehicle: profiles deliver both the approvals AND many restrictions. The endpoint agent and the MDM are complementary controls \u2014 know which one owns each setting, or they fight."],
"TUNING NOTES (macOS)\n  cert rules: Apple/developer identities\n  OS upgrades: policy events (pre-approve via Apple identity)\n  capability first: FDA missing -> 'policy broken' is really 'blind agent'\n  MDM + agent: complementary; know who owns what",
"Tune macOS policy with identity rules for Apple and vendor components, treat OS upgrades as policy events with pre-approvals, and check capability (approvals, version) before blaming the policy.",
["Hash rules on App-Store apps \u2014 the channel churns; use identity rules.",
"OS upgrade without policy prep \u2014 the Monday-after-upgrade ticket avalanche.",
"Blaming policy for missing prerequisites \u2014 check capability first."],
"Plan the macOS Sonoma-to-next upgrade as a policy event: what pre-approvals, what comms, what rollback?"),
L("macos-agents", "Troubleshooting macOS agents",
["'Installed but blind': check approvals FIRST (System Settings \u2192 Privacy & Security \u2192 Full Disk Access; system extensions). The agent's own status page usually names the missing grant \u2014 read it before reinstalling.",
"'Checks in but policy doesn't apply': capability vs configuration \u2014 verify the agent CAN enforce (approvals, version) before assuming the policy is wrong. Then check policy assignment (right group? right token?).",
"'Blocks Apple system apps': usually an OS-upgrade hash churn or an over-tight baseline. Fix with Apple-identity rules, not per-hash whack-a-mole \u2014 and pre-approve before the next OS upgrade.",
"'User approved the wrong thing': manual-approval environments produce creative misconfigurations. MDM profiles remove the user from the approval path \u2014 the structural fix for approval chaos."],
"MACOS TRIAGE\n  blind agent      -> approvals (FDA, sys ext)\n  policy not applying -> capability, then assignment\n  system apps blocked -> Apple-identity rules\n  approval chaos     -> MDM profiles (remove the user)",
"Triage macOS agents in order: approvals first (the blind agent), then capability versus assignment (policy not applying), then identity rules for system components. Manual-approval chaos gets the structural fix: MDM profiles.",
["Reinstalling before checking approvals \u2014 the reinstall inherits the missing grant.",
"Tuning policy for a capability problem \u2014 the policy was never the issue.",
"Per-hash fixes for OS components \u2014 Apple-identity rules, always."],
"Diagnose: 3 of 15 Macs check in but show 'limited capability.' Walk the triage aloud."),
]
