lx = {"id": "linux-agents", "name": "Linux Agents",
"description": "Endpoint protection on Linux: diverse distros, package-manager deployment, server-vs-workstation policy, and the CLI-native triage.",
"icon": "\U0001f427", "scenarios": []}
lx_lessons = [
L("linux-agents", "Linux deployment: distros and package managers",
["Linux is not one platform: Debian/Ubuntu (apt, .deb), RHEL-family (dnf/yum, .rpm), and the long tail. The agent ships per-family packages \u2014 your deployment must map each host to its family before pushing.",
"Deploy via the native package manager (repo + package install): it gives you versioning, dependencies, and clean uninstall for free. Curl-piped-to-bash installers are the emergency path, not the plan.",
"Configuration management (Ansible/Puppet/Chef-class tooling) is the Linux GPO: the agent install, the site token, and the service enablement all ride the existing automation. Greenfield Linux without config management gets it as part of this project.",
"Kernel compatibility: endpoint agents hook deep. Verify the agent supports your kernel versions BEFORE deploying \u2014 the 'it installed but the system is unstable' failure is a kernel-mismatch failure, and it's discovered in the pilot or in production."],
"LINUX DEPLOY MAP\n  debian/ubuntu: apt repo + .deb (ansible role)\n  rhel family:   dnf repo + .rpm (ansible role)\n  verify: kernel support matrix BEFORE pilot\n  config: token + service enable via automation",
"Map every host to its distro family, deploy through the native package manager via your automation, and verify the kernel support matrix before the pilot. The matrix check is what keeps 'installed but unstable' out of production.",
["One package for all distros \u2014 map hosts to families first.",
"Curl-bash as the plan \u2014 the emergency path, not the deployment.",
"Skipping the kernel matrix \u2014 instability discovered in production."],
"Map Harbor Dental's 2 lab Linux boxes: distro family, package, deploy method, kernel check."),
L("linux-agents", "Server vs workstation policy",
["Linux servers and workstations need different policies: SERVERS run few, stable, known services \u2014 the allowlist is tight, change is rare, and every change is a change ticket. WORKSTATIONS (dev machines) run diverse, churning toolchains \u2014 the policy needs the approval workflow to keep up.",
"Servers: default-deny with a minimal baseline (the service set + management tooling), ringfencing on the service accounts, and NO interactive-user exceptions. A server is not a workstation; don't policy it like one.",
"Dev workstations: certificate/hash rules for the toolchain managers (not every package they install \u2014 approve the MANAGERS, ringfence what they spawn), plus a fast approval path for the long tail. The policy enables the workflow; it doesn't enumerate it.",
"Containers: the agent sees the host; container escapes are the threat model. Policy the container runtime tightly (what it may spawn, what it may mount, where it may reach) \u2014 the runtime is the highest-value process on the box."],
"POLICY SPLIT\n  servers:      minimal baseline, ticketed change, no user exceptions\n  workstations: toolchain managers approved, fast approval for the tail\n  containers:   runtime tightly ringfenced (spawn/mount/network)",
"Split the policy by role: minimal ticketed baselines for servers, toolchain-manager approvals plus a fast lane for dev workstations, and the tightest leashes on the container runtime. One policy for both gives servers holes and workstations friction.",
["One policy for servers and workstations \u2014 the server gets holes, the workstation gets friction.",
"Enumerating every dev package \u2014 approve the managers, ringfence the rest.",
"Unwatched container runtime \u2014 the highest-value process needs the tightest leash."],
"Write the policy split for: 2 lab servers, 3 dev workstations, 1 container host."),
L("linux-agents", "CLI-native triage on Linux",
["Linux triage lives in the shell: the agent's CLI shows status, policy version, and recent denies; the system logs (journalctl) show the agent's own health; and the standard toolkit (ps, ss, systemctl) shows what the box is actually doing.",
"The triage order: agent status (is it running? enforcing? current policy?) \u2192 agent's deny log (what was blocked, which rule?) \u2192 system state (what's actually running/listening?) \u2192 the verdict. Capability before policy, always.",
"Script the routine: a 10-line health script (agent status, check-in age, policy version, deny count by hour) run daily beats ad-hoc SSH heroics. Operators who script the routine have time for the anomalies.",
"Logs are the audit trail: forward the agent's logs to the SIEM/syslog aggregator. An endpoint whose logs stay local is unmonitored \u2014 the 'we'll check the box if something happens' plan."],
"LINUX TRIAGE\n  1. agent status: running? enforcing? policy current?\n  2. agent denies: what blocked, which rule?\n  3. system state: ps/ss/systemctl reality check\n  4. verdict: capability, then policy, then action",
"Triage from the shell in order: agent status and policy currency, the agent's deny log, then the system reality check with standard tools. Script the routine into a daily health check \u2014 automate the routine, save yourself for the anomalies.",
["SSH heroics instead of scripted checks \u2014 automate the routine.",
"Logs staying local \u2014 unforwarded logs are unmonitored logs.",
"Triage from the ticket instead of the agent CLI \u2014 the box is the source."],
"Write the 10-line Linux agent health script: status, check-in age, policy version, deny counts."),
L("linux-agents", "Linux-specific gotchas",
["SELinux/AppArmor interact with the agent: a 'blocked' event might be the MAC system, not your policy. Know which layer decided \u2014 the audit logs of each name their decider. Triage the right control.",
"Immutable/mutable paths: /usr is managed (package manager), /opt and /usr/local are the wild west (manual installs), /tmp and /dev/shm are executable-danger zones. Path rules must respect this map \u2014 /tmp execution is the classic Linux hole.",
"Service accounts: Linux servers run many daemons as distinct users. Ringfence by service identity where the platform supports it \u2014 'the web server user may not spawn shells' is the Linux app-to-app leash.",
"Upgrades: distro major upgrades (like macOS major versions) churn hashes and behaviors. Treat them as policy events: pre-approve, pilot, communicate \u2014 the same discipline, different package manager."],
"LINUX GOTCHA MAP\n  MAC layer: SELinux/AppArmor may decide first (check whose audit)\n  paths: /usr managed | /opt,/usr/local wild | /tmp,/dev/shm danger\n  services: ringfence by service user where supported\n  upgrades: policy events (pre-approve, pilot, comms)",
"Keep the gotcha map handy: the MAC layer may decide before your policy, /tmp and /dev/shm are executable-danger zones, services get ringfenced by user, and distro upgrades are policy events. Check the decider first.",
["Blaming your policy for SELinux's deny \u2014 check the decider.",
"Path rules ignoring /tmp executability \u2014 the classic hole.",
"Major upgrade without policy prep \u2014 the Monday ticket avalanche, Linux edition."],
"Diagnose: '/opt/vendor/bin/tool blocked after apt upgrade.' Which gotcha, and what's the fix?"),
]
for _l in lx_lessons: new_lessons.append(_l)
