# ---------------- uninstall-protection ----------------
up = {"id": "uninstall-protection", "name": "Uninstall Protection",
"description": "Keeping the agent on the endpoint: tamper protection, uninstall authorization workflows, and detecting removal attempts.",
"icon": "\U0001f512", "scenarios": []}
up_lessons = [
L("uninstall-protection", "Why uninstall protection exists",
["An endpoint agent the user can remove is a suggestion, not a control. Attackers' first move on a protected box is killing the protection; users' first move against friction is the same. Tamper protection exists because both try.",
"The threat model: malware with admin rights attempts service-stop, driver-unload, or uninstall. The user model: the frustrated employee following a forum post. The control must resist both \u2014 authorization, not obscurity.",
"Uninstall protection has three layers: TAMPER resistance (can't kill/stop without authorization), UNINSTALL authorization (removal needs a ticket + code/approval), and DETECTION (attempts are logged and alerted \u2014 the attempt itself is a signal).",
"A 'protected' endpoint whose agent was silently removed is worse than a known-unprotected one: the console shows stale green while the box is naked. Detection of removal is as important as prevention."],
"PROTECTION LAYERS\n  tamper:    can't stop/disable without auth\n  uninstall: removal needs ticket + authorization\n  detect:    attempts logged + alerted (the attempt is signal)",
"Assume both attackers and frustrated users will try to remove the agent: build tamper resistance, require authorized workflows for removal, and alert on attempts. A silently removed agent showing stale-green is worse than a known-unprotected box.",
["Relying on obscurity ('users don't know how') \u2014 forums exist.",
"Prevention without detection \u2014 the silent removal is the worst outcome.",
"Stale-green consoles \u2014 removed agents must alarm, not fade."],
"Check your own posture: can a standard user stop the agent? An admin? What alerts fire?"),
L("uninstall-protection", "The authorized uninstall workflow",
["Legitimate uninstalls happen: machine decommission, agent migration, troubleshooting. The workflow: TICKET (reason, machine, requester), AUTHORIZATION (approver, not the requester), METHOD (the supported removal path \u2014 console command or authorized local tool), VERIFICATION (console confirms removal, asset record updated).",
"Self-service uninstall is never the answer: 'users can remove it themselves for troubleshooting' converts every frustrated user into an unprotected endpoint. The helpdesk performs authorized removals \u2014 users request them.",
"The authorization code/token model: time-limited, single-machine, logged. A universal uninstall password is a shared secret \u2014 it leaks, and then every machine is one forum post from naked.",
"Decommissioning ties in: the uninstall workflow's final step updates the asset record and removes the console entry. The 'decommissioned ghosts' from the deployment domain are uninstall-workflow failures."],
"UNINSTALL WORKFLOW\n  ticket:  reason + machine + requester\n  auth:    approver (not requester) + time-limited token\n  method:  console/supported path (never self-service)\n  verify:  console confirms + asset record updated",
"Route every legitimate removal through ticket, approver, time-limited token, and verification \u2014 never self-service. The workflow's final step updates the asset record, which is what keeps the ghost fleet from forming.",
["Self-service uninstall \u2014 every frustrated user becomes an exception.",
"Universal uninstall passwords \u2014 shared secrets leak; scope the token.",
"Skipping verification \u2014 the ghost fleet is born here."],
"Write Harbor Dental's uninstall workflow: who approves, what token model, how verification works."),
L("uninstall-protection", "Tamper events: detection and response",
["Tamper attempts are high-signal events: service-stop attempts, driver-unload attempts, policy-modification attempts, uninstall attempts. Each is logged; each should ALERT \u2014 not sit in a log.",
"The triage: WHO attempted (user? admin? SYSTEM?), WHAT was attempted (stop? uninstall? modify?), WHEN (3 AM attempts differ from 10 AM), and the CONTEXT (troubleshooting ticket open? known incident?).",
"Legitimate tamper exists: the helpdesk's authorized troubleshooting, the migration team's scripted removal. The difference between legitimate and hostile tamper is the ticket \u2014 authorized tamper is ticketed tamper.",
"Response to hostile tamper: isolate the endpoint (the tamper attempt may be step one of an attack), investigate the actor and the motive, verify the agent is intact and enforcing. A tamper attempt is a detection \u2014 work it like one."],
"TAMPER TRIAGE\n  who:  user/admin/SYSTEM?\n  what: stop/uninstall/modify?\n  when: 3 AM vs 10 AM (context matters)\n  ticket: authorized tamper is TICKETED tamper\n  hostile: isolate + investigate + verify agent intact",
"Alert on every tamper attempt and triage who, what, when, and whether a ticket exists: ticketed troubleshooting is legitimate, 3 AM SYSTEM uninstalls are incident-grade. The attempt itself is the signal \u2014 work it.",
["Tamper logs without alerts \u2014 the signal sits unread.",
"Treating all tamper as hostile \u2014 ticketed troubleshooting is legitimate; check first.",
"Treating all tamper as benign \u2014 the 3 AM SYSTEM uninstall attempt is not troubleshooting."],
"Write the tamper alert runbook: alert text, triage steps, and the isolate-vs-monitor decision."),
L("uninstall-protection", "The admin problem: protecting from administrators",
["Local admins CAN remove most software \u2014 that's what admin means. Uninstall protection against admins relies on: the agent's self-defense (tamper resistance even from admin), the authorization workflow (admin still needs the ticket+token), and detection (admin tamper is the highest-priority alert).",
"Reduce the need: fewer local admins (the elevation domain's whole point) means fewer actors who CAN tamper. Every removed standing admin is one fewer tamper-capable account.",
"Break-glass exists: domain-level or vendor-assisted removal for true emergencies (ransomware encrypting THROUGH the agent, agent causing boot failure). Break-glass is documented, rare, audited \u2014 and its use triggers a review.",
"The honest statement: no software uninstall protection survives a determined admin with physical access and time. The control raises the cost and \u2014 critically \u2014 makes the attempt VISIBLE. Visibility is the real protection."],
"ADMIN REALITY\n  admins CAN tamper -> self-defense + workflow + detection\n  fewer admins    -> fewer tamper-capable actors\n  break-glass     -> documented, rare, audited, reviewed\n  honest limit    -> the attempt becomes VISIBLE",
"Design for admins' real capability: self-defense plus the ticket-and-token workflow plus detection, with fewer standing admins overall. Document break-glass for true emergencies \u2014 and remember the honest limit: visibility is the real protection.",
["Pretending admins can't tamper \u2014 design for the real capability.",
"No break-glass plan \u2014 the emergency then improvises one.",
"Treating visibility as secondary \u2014 visibility IS the protection against admins."],
"Assess your environment: how many local admins, what tamper alerts exist, is break-glass documented?"),
]
for _l in up_lessons: new_lessons.append(_l)
up_scenarios = [
S("tlup-01", "uninstall-protection", "The forum-post uninstall",
"Three users in one week: each disabled the agent following the same forum post ('fix slow PC: disable your security'). All three machines were unprotected for days; one now shows ransomware indicators. The assessor asks for the full response.",
["tamper", "user-behavior", "response"],
[["The immediate", "One machine shows ransomware indicators: isolate it NOW, treat as an incident. The other two: verify clean, re-enable, investigate the unprotected window."],
["The control failure", "Standard users could disable the agent: tamper protection is misconfigured or absent. The forum post worked because the control allowed it."],
["The systemic", "Fix tamper protection (standard users cannot disable, period), alert on disable attempts, and address the 'slow PC' complaints that drove users to the forum \u2014 the bypass had a motive; fix the motive too."]],
"Full response?",
["Punish the three users for disabling protection",
"Isolate the ransomware-indicator machine as an incident; verify and re-enable the other two with window investigation; fix tamper protection so standard users cannot disable; alert on attempts; and address the performance complaints that motivated the bypass \u2014 no punishment for the users, fix the control and the motive",
"Re-enable the agents and move on",
"Block the forum site"],
1,
"Three users, one forum post, one ransomware indicator. What failed \u2014 the users, or the control that let users disable it?",
"Correct is B: it prioritizes correctly (incident first), fixes the actual control failure (user-disable possible), adds detection, addresses the motive (slow PC), and refuses punishment (which teaches concealment). A is wrong: punishment blinds you to the next bypass. C is wrong: re-enabling without fixing tamper protection or investigating the windows changes nothing. D is wrong: blocking one forum doesn't un-teach the method or fix the control.",
7),
S("tlup-02", "uninstall-protection", "The 3 AM SYSTEM uninstall attempt",
"Alert: 3:12 AM, server SRV-DB-02, uninstall attempt on the agent by NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM, blocked by tamper protection. No ticket, no maintenance window. The assessor asks for your read.",
["tamper-detection", "anomaly", "incident"],
[["The signals", "3 AM + server + SYSTEM context + uninstall attempt + no ticket + no window: the tamper attempt used the highest-privilege context at the quietest hour with no authorization."],
["The read", "SYSTEM-context uninstall attempts are not users troubleshooting: this is malware's standard first move (kill the protection) or a compromised maintenance script. Either way, incident-grade."],
["The response", "Isolate the server, verify the agent is intact and enforcing, investigate what runs as SYSTEM (scheduled tasks, services), hunt for the delivery \u2014 the blocked attempt is a detection of an active attack step."]],
"Your read and response?",
["The tamper protection worked; no action needed",
"Incident-grade: the 3 AM SYSTEM-context uninstall attempt with no ticket is malware's standard opening move \u2014 isolate the server, verify agent integrity, investigate SYSTEM-context actors (tasks, services), hunt the delivery; the blocked attempt is a detection, not a closed event",
"Whitelist the SYSTEM uninstall for maintenance",
"Reboot the server"],
1,
"Blocked by tamper protection at 3 AM from SYSTEM with no ticket. Is the story 'control worked' \u2014 or 'attack detected'?",
"Correct is B: it reads the signal cluster correctly (this is the attack pattern, not a maintenance pattern), responds as an incident, and treats the block as the detection it is. A is wrong: 'worked, closed' ignores that the attempt proves an active hostile actor on a database server. C is wrong: whitelisting SYSTEM uninstalls for 'maintenance' creates the exact hole the attacker wants. D is wrong: rebooting does nothing about the actor that attempted the uninstall.",
7),
S("tlup-03", "uninstall-protection", "The migration's scripted removal",
"Migration project: 200 agents must be removed (vendor switch). The project plan says 'technicians will uninstall manually per machine.' The assessor asks for your review of the plan.",
["migration", "scale", "workflow"],
[["The plan's flaw", "200 manual uninstalls: unscalable, unauditable, unverifiable \u2014 and each manual removal is a tamper-protection interaction performed by a human under time pressure."],
["The right method", "Scripted authorized removal: console-driven or token-based mass uninstall, logged per machine, with verification (console confirms each removal). The workflow scales; humans don't."],
["The sequencing", "Removal verified BEFORE new-agent install per machine (never unprotected-and-new-agent-less), in rings like any change, with the rollback being 'reinstall the old agent' \u2014 tested."]],
"Your review?",
["Approve the manual plan; technicians are careful",
"Reject manual-per-machine: use scripted authorized removal (console/token-driven, logged per machine, verified per removal), sequenced in rings with removal-verified-before-install and a tested rollback \u2014 200 manual uninstalls is 200 un-audited tamper events",
"Skip removal; install the new agent over the old",
"Let users uninstall it themselves to save time"],
1,
"Two hundred manual uninstalls. What is each one, from the tamper-protection log's perspective?",
"Correct is B: it identifies the scale/audit problem, prescribes the scripted authorized method, and sequences it safely. A is wrong: 'careful technicians' doesn't scale to 200 or produce an audit trail. C is wrong: installing over the old agent creates the conflict failure from the deployment domain. D is wrong: user self-service uninstall is the exact anti-pattern the domain prohibits.",
6),
S("tlup-04", "uninstall-protection", "The admin who 'needed' to disable it",
"A domain admin disabled the agent on their workstation 'for troubleshooting' \u2014 no ticket, no token, and the tamper protection allowed it (admin bypass configured 'for IT convenience'). The machine was unprotected for 6 hours. The assessor asks for findings.",
["admin-bypass", "tamper", "convenience"],
[["Finding 1", "Admin bypass on tamper protection 'for convenience': the control was configured to not control the most capable actors. The bypass is the finding."],
["Finding 2", "Unticketed 6-hour unprotected window on a domain admin's workstation: the highest-value target, unmonitored, by its owner's hand."],
["The fix", "Remove the admin bypass (admins use the ticket+token workflow like everyone), alert on ALL tamper events including admin-initiated, and investigate the 6-hour window. Convenience is not a control rationale."]],
"Findings and fix?",
["The admin was troubleshooting; no issue",
"Two findings: the tamper protection's admin bypass ('for convenience') nullifies the control for the most capable actors, and a 6-hour unticketed unprotected window on a domain admin workstation \u2014 remove the bypass, require the workflow for admins too, alert on all tamper, investigate the window",
"Remove the admin's rights as punishment",
"The agent is too restrictive; loosen it"],
1,
"'For IT convenience' \u2014 what does that phrase actually authorize, and for whom?",
"Correct is B: it names both findings, removes the bypass (the structural fix), and investigates the window. A is wrong: unticketed protection-disablement by a domain admin is never 'no issue.' C is wrong: punishment without fixing the bypass leaves the next admin the same hole. D is wrong: the agent wasn't restrictive \u2014 it was correctly protective until the bypass neutered it.",
6),
S("tlup-05", "uninstall-protection", "The stale-green console",
"Incident review: a machine was compromised 3 weeks ago. The console showed it 'healthy' the entire time \u2014 because the agent was uninstalled on day 1, and the console displays last-known state indefinitely. The assessor asks for the systemic failure.",
["stale-state", "detection", "console-hygiene"],
[["The failure", "The console reports absence as health: an uninstalled agent renders as 'healthy (stale)' instead of alarming. The monitoring displays the opposite of reality."],
["The cost", "3 weeks of assumed protection on a compromised machine: the incident's dwell time was manufactured by the console's silence."],
["The fix", "Stale-agent alerting (no check-in for X hours = alert, not green), distinct console states (healthy / stale / uninstalled / never-seen), and the rule: absence of signal is a signal. Green must mean verified-current."]],
"Systemic failure and fix?",
["The console is fine; the techs should have noticed",
"Systemic failure: the console displays uninstalled agents as healthy-stale, manufacturing 3 weeks of false assurance \u2014 fix with stale-agent alerting, distinct agent states, and the principle that absence of signal is itself a signal",
"Check consoles more often",
"The agent should be harder to uninstall"],
1,
"Three weeks 'healthy,' agent gone day one. What did the console actually report \u2014 and what should it have reported?",
"Correct is B: it identifies the display failure as systemic (not human), prescribes the alerting and state model, and states the principle. A is wrong: 'should have noticed' blames humans for a console that lied. C is wrong: more frequent checks of a lying console just observe the lie more often. D is wrong: harder uninstall doesn't fix the DISPLAY failure \u2014 a legitimately uninstalled agent would still show green.",
6),
S("tlup-06", "uninstall-protection", "The break-glass request",
"2 AM: ransomware is encrypting THROUGH the agent on a critical server (agent malfunction suspected). The on-call admin requests break-glass removal: 'I need the documented emergency uninstall NOW.' The assessor asks how break-glass should work.",
["break-glass", "emergency", "authorization"],
[["When break-glass is legitimate", "This is the case it exists for: the protection itself is implicated in an active incident, and standard removal is too slow or unavailable."],
["How it must work", "Documented procedure + emergency authorization (two-person, even at 2 AM \u2014 wake someone) + time-limited effect + FULL audit (who, what, why, when) + mandatory post-incident review of the break-glass use itself."],
["The guardrail", "Break-glass is rare, audited, and reviewed \u2014 every use triggers 'was this truly break-glass, or did the normal workflow fail?' If the normal workflow failed, fix THAT."]],
"How should break-glass work here?",
["Deny it; break-glass is too dangerous to ever use",
"Grant it under the documented emergency procedure: two-person authorization, time-limited, fully audited \u2014 then mandatory post-incident review of both the incident AND whether the break-glass use was truly necessary (or the normal workflow failed)",
"Give the admin the universal uninstall password",
"Approve it verbally and document later"],
1,
"The protection is implicated in an active incident at 2 AM. What makes break-glass SAFE to have \u2014 and safe to use?",
"Correct is B: it grants the legitimate emergency use under full guardrails (two-person, audited, time-limited) and \u2014 the maturity signal \u2014 reviews the break-glass use itself afterward. A is wrong: a break-glass that can never be used is a decoration; this is its exact use case. C is wrong: a universal password is the shared-secret failure. D is wrong: 'document later' for the highest-risk action is how accountability dies.",
6),
]
up["scenarios"] = up_scenarios
new_domains.append(up)

# ---------------- approval-center ----------------
ac = {"id": "approval-center", "name": "Approval Center Ops",
"description": "Running the request queue: intake, evidence, decisions, SLAs, and turning the queue from a complaint box into a program instrument.",
"icon": "\U0001f3ab", "scenarios": []}
ac_lessons = [
L("approval-center", "The queue is the job",
["The Approval Center is where the security program meets its users: every 'I need this app' arrives here. Staff it, measure it, and respect it \u2014 or the organization routes around you.",
"Request lifecycle: INTAKE (who, what binary, business need, ticket) \u2192 EVIDENCE (hash, signer, prevalence, sandbox if unknown) \u2192 DECISION (approve with rule+scope, or deny with reason) \u2192 COMMUNICATE (plain-language verdict + path to yes on denials).",
"Intake quality determines everything: a request with 'the file, the need, the ticket' decides in minutes; a request with 'it doesn't work' takes hours of back-and-forth. Invest in the intake form \u2014 it pays for itself daily.",
"The queue is also the program's sensor: request VOLUME by category shows where the baseline is thin; DECISION patterns show where policy is wrong; REPEAT requests show where the process failed the user. Read it like an instrument."],
"REQUEST LIFECYCLE\n  intake -> evidence -> decision -> communicate\n  good intake: file + need + ticket (decides in minutes)\n  bad intake:  'it doesn't work' (hours of back-and-forth)\n  the queue is a sensor: read volume, decisions, repeats",
"Run the request lifecycle end to end \u2014 intake, evidence, decision, communication \u2014 and read the queue as an instrument: volume shows baseline gaps, decisions show policy errors, repeats show process failures.",
["Treating the queue as a complaint box \u2014 it is the program's user interface.",
"Skipping communication on denials \u2014 silent denies breed shadow IT.",
"Ignoring what the queue's patterns say about the policy \u2014 the sensor is talking; listen."],
"Design Harbor Dental's intake form: fields, required vs optional, and the auto-triage rules."),
L("approval-center", "SLA: the usability contract",
["The SLA is the program's usability contract: STANDARD requests decided in 4 business hours, URGENT (with evidence) in 1 hour. Published, staffed, measured \u2014 a promise the organization can plan around.",
"Urgency needs a definition, not a feeling: 'business function blocked with deadline today' is urgent; 'would be nice today' is standard. The intake form captures the urgency claim; the triage verifies it.",
"Breach protocol: when the SLA slips, the queue gets triaged (urgent-with-evidence first), staffing flexes, and stakeholders get a status note \u2014 not silence. SLA breaches communicated are incidents; uncommunicated, they're betrayals.",
"The SLA is also the anti-shadow-IT control: users tolerate a 4-hour wait they can see; they route around a black hole. Publish the queue depth if you must \u2014 visibility is trust."],
"SLA CARD\n  standard: 4 business hours (published)\n  urgent:   1 hour (with evidence; urgency verified)\n  breach:   triage + flex staffing + status note\n  the SLA is the anti-shadow-IT control",
"Publish the SLA, define urgency crisply, staff to the promise, and communicate breaches instead of hiding them. The SLA is the anti-shadow-IT control: users tolerate a visible 4-hour wait; they route around a black hole.",
["Unpublished SLA \u2014 users experience it as 'security doesn't care.'",
"Urgency on demand \u2014 verify the claim, or everything is urgent.",
"Silent breaches \u2014 communicate, or trust dies quietly."],
"Write Harbor Dental's SLA card: times, urgency definition, breach protocol, staffing."),
L("approval-center", "Decision quality at speed",
["Speed and quality coexist via the 90-second triage: SIGNER? HASH? PREVALENCE? NEED ON RECORD? \u2014 four questions, then the tree: approve (proper rule), sandbox (unknown), or deny (with reason + path to yes).",
"Precedent accelerates: the tenth request for the same vendor's tool should decide faster than the first \u2014 because the first built the precedent (signer verified, rule pattern set). Document precedents; don't re-derive them.",
"Batch the routine: recurring vendor updates, known-good internal tools, standard department apps \u2014 these get fast-lane handling (verify-then-approve), reserving analyst time for the genuinely ambiguous.",
"Quality gates that don't slow: every decision still gets the record (what, evidence, verdict, scope) \u2014 the record IS the quality control, and it takes 60 seconds. Speed never skips the record."],
"90-SECOND TRIAGE\n  signer? hash? prevalence? need on record?\n  -> approve (proper rule) / sandbox / deny+path-to-yes\n  precedent: 10th same-vendor request rides the 1st's work\n  every decision: the 60-second record",
"Decide fast without deciding sloppy: the 90-second triage, precedent records for repeat vendors, fast lanes for the routine \u2014 and the 60-second decision record on every call. Speed never skips the record.",
["Re-deriving every decision from scratch \u2014 build precedent, ride it.",
"Fast-lane without verification \u2014 'known vendor' still gets the hash check.",
"Skipping the record for speed \u2014 the record is the quality gate."],
"Run the 90-second triage on three invented requests: one approve, one sandbox, one deny."),
L("approval-center", "The deny with a path to yes",
["A deny without a path to yes is a bypass waiting to happen: the user still needs the outcome, and now they need it outside your visibility. Every deny must include the concrete next step.",
"The path shapes: 'bring me the signed build from the pipeline' (unknown \u2192 verifiable), 'submit the business justification with manager approval' (need unclear \u2192 documented), 'use the approved alternative X' (risky tool \u2192 safe substitute).",
"Plain language, not policy citations: 'I can't approve this unsigned binary because we can't verify who made it \u2014 if your vendor provides the signed version, that's a 10-minute approval.' The user hears help, not bureaucracy.",
"Track deny-to-approval conversion: what fraction of denies come back with the missing evidence and get approved? High conversion means the paths work; low conversion means the paths are theater \u2014 or the denies were wrong."],
"DENY TEMPLATE\n  verdict:  DENY (unsigned, unverifiable publisher)\n  why:      plain-language reason (one sentence)\n  path:     'signed build from the pipeline -> 10-min approval'\n  metric:   deny-to-approval conversion rate",
"Close every deny with a concrete next step in plain language, and measure deny-to-approval conversion to prove the paths work. A deny without a path is a bypass waiting to happen.",
["Deny with no path \u2014 the bypass factory.",
"Policy-citation denies \u2014 users hear bureaucracy, not help.",
"Paths that don't work \u2014 measure conversion; fix theater paths."],
"Write deny-with-path responses for: unsigned vendor tool, unneeded-but-wanted app, and a duplicate request."),
]
for _l in ac_lessons: new_lessons.append(_l)
ac_scenarios = [
S("tlac-01", "approval-center", "The 120-ticket Monday",
"Monday 9 AM: 120 pending requests, oldest 11 days. Three departments now use 'VP email approval' instead. The helpdesk lead asks for the recovery plan.",
["queue-health", "sla", "shadow-it"],
[["The diagnosis", "Unstaffed, unmeasured, no-SLA queue: the 11-day oldest is the metric. The organization routed around the failure with shadow approvals."],
["The triage", "Sort by urgency-with-evidence first (real deadlines), then by age; kill duplicates; fast-lane the precedented. The backlog is triaged like an incident \u2014 because it is one."],
["The systemic", "Publish the SLA, staff to it, absorb the shadow demand into the real process (the demand was legitimate), and add queue-depth alerting so 120 never accumulates silently again."]],
"Recovery plan?",
["Hire three more approvers today",
"Triage the backlog like an incident (urgent-with-evidence first, dedupe, fast-lane precedent), then publish and staff the SLA, absorb the legitimate shadow demand into the real workflow, and add queue-depth alerting",
"Auto-approve everything over 7 days old",
"Ban VP-email approvals immediately"],
1,
"One hundred twenty tickets, eleven days, shadow process running. Is this a staffing problem \u2014 or a process failure wearing a queue?",
"Correct is B: it triages the incident first, then fixes the process (SLA, staffing, absorption of real demand), and adds the alerting. A is wrong: hiring into a process without SLA or triage scales the failure. C is wrong: auto-approving aged requests converts backlog into unscoped decisions. D is wrong: banning the workaround without fixing the queue recreates it in weeks.",
7),
S("tlac-02", "approval-center", "The intake form from nowhere",
"Half the queue is 'it doesn't work' with no binary name, no error, no user context. Analysts spend hours per request on back-and-forth. The assessor asks for the fix.",
["intake", "efficiency", "process-design"],
[["The cost", "Each bad-intake request costs hours of analyst back-and-forth: the queue's throughput is throttled by its intake, not its analysts."],
["The fix", "Required intake fields: binary name/path, the exact block message or screenshot, business need, deadline, requester, ticket. Plus auto-triage: known-good precedents fast-lane, unknowns route to sandbox scheduling."],
["The principle", "Intake quality is a design choice: every required field is analyst-hours saved. The form is the cheapest analyst you'll ever hire."]],
"Fix?",
["Hire more analysts for the back-and-forth",
"Redesign intake: required fields (binary, block evidence, need, deadline, ticket) plus auto-triage rules (precedent fast-lane, unknown \u2192 sandbox) \u2014 intake quality is throughput",
"Reject all bad-intake requests",
"Call every requester for details"],
1,
"Half the queue is 'it doesn't work.' Is the bottleneck the analysts \u2014 or the intake?",
"Correct is B: it fixes the throughput constraint at its source (intake design) with both fields and auto-triage. A is wrong: more analysts doing back-and-forth scales the waste. C is wrong: mass rejection punishes users for the form's failure. D is wrong: calling every requester IS the back-and-forth, formalized.",
5),
S("tlac-03", "approval-center", "The precedent nobody wrote down",
"Same vendor tool, 15th request this quarter. Each was approved after a full 2-hour investigation \u2014 because no one documented the first one's findings. The assessor asks what the waste is and how to stop it.",
["precedent", "knowledge-management", "efficiency"],
[["The waste", "14 redundant investigations: the signer was verified once (in effect, 15 times), the rule pattern re-derived each time. Hours of analyst time for zero new information."],
["The fix", "Precedent records: vendor verified (signer, date, by whom), rule pattern set, fast-lane criteria defined ('same signer + same product line \u2192 verify hash, approve'). The 16th request takes 10 minutes."],
["The systemic", "The approval record template includes a 'precedent' field: each decision notes whether it establishes or follows precedent. Knowledge compounds instead of evaporating."]],
"Waste and fix?",
["The investigations were thorough; no waste",
"Waste is 14 redundant re-investigations: create precedent records (verified signer, rule pattern, fast-lane criteria) so repeat requests ride the first one's work \u2014 and add 'precedent' to the decision record template",
"Stop approving the tool to end the requests",
"Assign all 15 to one analyst for consistency"],
1,
"Fifteen requests, fifteen full investigations, one vendor. What was re-derived fourteen times \u2014 and where should it have lived?",
"Correct is B: it quantifies the waste, creates the precedent mechanism, and templates it into the record. A is wrong: thoroughness repeated 15x without learning is toil, not rigor. C is wrong: denying a legitimate tool to save investigation time punishes the business for the knowledge gap. D is wrong: one analyst doing 15 redundant investigations is still 15 redundant investigations.",
5),
S("tlac-04", "approval-center", "The urgent that wasn't",
"'URGENT' request: 'need this game installed for the team morale event Friday.' Binary: unsigned, from a personal file share. The requester marked it urgent and CC'd their manager. The assessor asks how urgency gets verified.",
["urgency", "verification", "triage"],
[["The urgency claim", "'Morale event Friday' is a deadline, not a business urgency: no business function is blocked. Urgency definitions exist precisely for this."],
["The binary", "Unsigned from a personal share: the evidence side is also red. Urgent-or-not, this binary needs the sandbox and the provenance check."],
["The response", "Reclassify to standard (the urgency definition decides, not the caps lock), sandbox the binary per the unknown workflow, and communicate both plainly: 'I've queued this as standard \u2014 here's why, and here's the path.'"]],
"How do you handle it?",
["Treat as urgent; the manager was CC'd",
"Verify against the urgency definition (no business function blocked \u2192 standard), run the unknown-binary workflow (sandbox + provenance) regardless of the flag, and communicate the reclassification plainly",
"Deny it for the caps lock",
"Approve it; morale matters"],
1,
"'URGENT' in caps, manager CC'd, game binary from a file share. What verifies urgency \u2014 the formatting, or the definition?",
"Correct is B: it applies the urgency definition (not the formatting), runs the evidence workflow the binary requires, and communicates without condescension. A is wrong: CC'ing a manager doesn't convert a morale event into business urgency. C is wrong: denying for tone is unprofessional \u2014 decide on evidence and definitions. D is wrong: 'morale' doesn't verify an unsigned file-share binary.",
5),
S("tlac-05", "approval-center", "The deny that created shadow IT",
"Three months ago: a data-analysis tool was denied ('not on the approved list') with no explanation and no path. Today: the team runs it on personal laptops, emailing datasets to themselves. The assessor asks for the causal chain.",
["deny-quality", "shadow-it", "consequences"],
[["The chain", "Unexplained deny \u2192 legitimate need unmet \u2192 work moves off-managed (personal laptops, email exfiltration of datasets) \u2014 the deny didn't stop the tool; it moved the DATA outside every control."],
["The irony", "The control designed to protect data caused the data's exfiltration: the deny's missing path-to-yes is the incident's root cause."],
["The fix", "Immediate: engage the team (what do they need? sanction a safe path \u2014 approved tool, VDI, whatever fits). Systemic: every deny carries the path to yes; measure deny-to-approval conversion; treat shadow IT as unmet-need signal."]],
"Causal chain and fix?",
["Punish the team for shadow IT",
"The unexplained deny moved legitimate work \u2014 and the datasets \u2014 outside all controls: engage the team to sanction a safe path now, and systemically require path-to-yes on every deny while treating shadow IT as unmet-need signal",
"Block personal laptops from the network",
"The deny was correct; the team was wrong"],
1,
"The deny 'worked' \u2014 and the datasets now live in personal email. What did the deny actually accomplish?",
"Correct is B: it traces the full causal chain (deny \u2192 unmet need \u2192 exfiltration), fixes forward (sanctioned safe path), and repairs the deny process. A is wrong: punishment for a need the process failed to serve teaches concealment. C is wrong: blocking personal laptops doesn't recall the emailed datasets. D is wrong: 'correct deny' with no path is what CAUSED the incident \u2014 correctness without usability is the failure.",
6),
S("tlac-06", "approval-center", "The metrics review",
"Quarterly: approval turnaround averaged 5.2h (SLA 4h), deny-to-approval conversion 70%, repeat-request rate 12%, queue depth never exceeded 30. The assessor asks: healthy or not, and what do you tune?",
["metrics", "sla", "tuning"],
[["The read", "Mostly healthy with one amber: turnaround 30% over SLA (mild breach), conversion 70% (paths work), repeats 12% (watch: some users loop), queue depth controlled (triage works)."],
["The tune", "Turnaround: find the slow segment (intake? evidence? approver availability?) \u2014 the breach is mild, so tune surgically. Repeats at 12%: sample them \u2014 are they the same apps (policy gap) or the same users (coaching)?"],
["The report", "'SLA amber at 5.2h vs 4h \u2014 investigating the slow segment; conversion healthy at 70%; repeats under review.' Honest, specific, owned \u2014 the metrics review the program, not the people."]],
"Healthy or not? What do you tune?",
["Healthy; 5.2h is close enough to 4h",
"Mostly healthy, one amber: turnaround 30% over SLA needs surgical tuning (find the slow segment), repeats at 12% need sampling (policy gap or coaching?) \u2014 report honestly with the investigation plan; conversion and queue depth are green",
"Unhealthy; overhaul the whole process",
"The SLA is too strict; relax it to 6h"],
1,
"5.2 vs 4.0, 70% conversion, 12% repeats. Which number is the amber \u2014 and what does 'tune' mean for it?",
"Correct is B: it reads each metric correctly, tunes surgically (not overhauls), and reports with the plan. A is wrong: 'close enough' normalizes the breach instead of investigating it. C is wrong: overhauling a mostly-healthy process for a 30% SLA miss is disproportionate. D is wrong: relaxing the SLA to meet the metric is grading your own homework.",
6),
]
ac["scenarios"] = ac_scenarios
new_domains.append(ac)

# ---------------- testing-environment ----------------
te = {"id": "testing-environment", "name": "Testing Environment",
"description": "Detonate before you decide: the sandbox workflow, reading detonation reports, and knowing the sandbox's limits.",
"icon": "\U0001f9ea", "scenarios": []}
te_lessons = [
L("testing-environment", "Detonate before you decide",
["The Testing Environment is the one-click sandbox: submit the unknown binary, get observed behavior \u2014 file/registry/network activity, AV-vendor flags, canary-file touches \u2014 in minutes. Unknown becomes observed; guessing becomes unprofessional.",
"The workflow: SUBMIT (the binary + context: where it came from, who requested it) \u2192 OBSERVE (read the full behavior report, not just the verdict) \u2192 DECIDE (clean + need \u2192 narrow rule; suspicious \u2192 deny with the report as evidence) \u2192 COMMUNICATE (the requester gets the plain-language version).",
"Submit with context: 'unsigned updater from vendor X, requested by Finance for month-end' detonates differently in your reading than the same binary with no story. The sandbox shows behavior; the context supplies meaning.",
"Speed matters: the sandbox's minutes-scale turnaround is what makes it the urgency-compatible evidence path. 'We can't wait' is never a reason to skip it \u2014 it's the reason to use it."],
"SANDBOX WORKFLOW\n  submit:  binary + provenance context\n  observe: full behavior report (not just verdict)\n  decide:  clean+need -> narrow rule | suspicious -> deny+report\n  communicate: plain-language verdict to requester",
"Make the sandbox the default for unknowns: submit with provenance context, read the behavior sections (not just the verdict), decide on observed behavior, and communicate plainly. Minutes of detonation beat hours of guessing.",
["Skipping the sandbox 'for speed' \u2014 it's the fastest evidence available.",
"Reading only the verdict \u2014 the behaviors ARE the decision inputs.",
"Submitting without context \u2014 behavior without provenance is half the story."],
"Write the sandbox submission for: unsigned 'Acme Updater 4.2', Finance, month-end. What context do you include?"),
L("testing-environment", "Reading the detonation report",
["The report's sections: EXECUTION (did it run? what did it spawn?), FILE (what did it read/write? canary files touched?), REGISTRY (persistence attempts? Run keys?), NETWORK (where did it reach? DNS patterns?), and VERDICT (the sandbox's summary \u2014 read last, not first).",
"Canary files are the tripwires: sandbox decoy documents/credentials. A binary that reads canary 'passwords.txt' is telling you its intent \u2014 no legitimate updater does that. Learn the canary vocabulary.",
"Network behavior is the richest signal: newly-registered domains, foreign ASNs, DNS tunneling patterns, encoded payloads. An 'updater' reaching a 3-day-old domain is not updating.",
"Correlate sections: file encryption + Run-key writes + C2 contact = ransomware pattern across three sections. Single-section anomalies get investigation; multi-section patterns get verdicts."],
"REPORT READING\n  execution: spawned what?\n  file:      canary touches? mass writes?\n  registry:  Run keys? services?\n  network:   new domains? foreign ASN? tunneling?\n  verdict:   read LAST (your read first)",
"Read execution, file, registry, and network sections before the verdict: canary touches reveal intent, network behavior reveals command-and-control, and multi-section patterns decide. Your read comes first; the summary confirms it.",
["Verdict-first reading \u2014 your analysis should precede the summary.",
"Ignoring canary touches \u2014 intent evidence, gift-wrapped.",
"Single-section conclusions \u2014 patterns across sections decide."],
"Read an invented report: updater touches canary passwords.txt + reaches new domain. Your verdict?"),
L("testing-environment", "Clean, suspicious, malicious: the decision",
["CLEAN + business need \u2192 narrow scoped rule: the sandbox observed benign behavior, the need is documented. The rule is still narrow (this binary, this scope) \u2014 clean sandbox is strong evidence, not a blank check.",
"SUSPICIOUS \u2192 deny with the report as evidence + investigate provenance: suspicious means observed bad behaviors. The requester gets the plain-language version ('it tried to read stored passwords'); the vendor gets a supply-chain question.",
"MALICIOUS \u2192 incident process: the binary is hostile, the requester may be a victim (where did they get it?), and the delivery path needs hunting. The sandbox just became a detection source.",
"Document the decision WITH the report attached: the next analyst (or auditor) replays your reasoning from the same evidence. 'Sandboxed, clean, approved' with the report linked is the complete record."],
"SANDBOX DECISIONS\n  clean + need  -> narrow rule (report attached)\n  suspicious    -> deny + report as evidence + provenance hunt\n  malicious     -> incident process (requester may be victim)",
"Map the report to the decision: clean plus need gets a narrow rule, suspicious gets a deny with the report as evidence plus a provenance hunt, malicious pivots to incident response. Attach the report to every decision.",
["Clean-sandbox blank checks \u2014 narrow rules even for clean results.",
"Suspicious without provenance hunt \u2014 where did the hostile binary come from?",
"Decisions without the attached report \u2014 the next analyst can't replay it."],
"Decide three invented reports: one clean, one suspicious, one malicious. Write each decision record."),
L("testing-environment", "The sandbox's limits",
["Evasive malware exists: sandbox-aware binaries that sleep, check for virtualization, or require user interaction the sandbox doesn't provide. A clean report is strong evidence \u2014 not proof. Combine with signer, reputation, and need.",
"Time-bombs: behavior scheduled past the detonation window. The sandbox watches minutes; some malware waits hours. For high-stakes decisions, extend the detonation or add the time-box to the rule.",
"Environment fidelity: the sandbox is a generic Windows image, not YOUR gold build. Behaviors that depend on your environment (domain logons, internal shares) won't manifest \u2014 interpret absence carefully.",
"The sandbox is one vote: strong, fast, and evidence-based \u2014 but one vote among signer verification, hash reputation, prevalence, and business need. Analysts who sandbox-and-stop miss the other signals."],
"SANDBOX LIMITS\n  evasion:    sleepers, VM checks, interaction-needed\n  time:       bombs past the window -> extend or time-box\n  fidelity:   generic image, not your gold build\n  verdict:    one strong vote, not the election",
"Respect the sandbox's boundaries: evasion techniques, time-bombs past the window, and generic-image fidelity limits mean a clean report is strong evidence, never proof. Extend the detonation or time-box the rule when the stakes warrant it.",
["Clean-report certainty \u2014 strong evidence, never proof.",
"Interpreting absence as benign \u2014 'didn't phone home' in a generic image proves little.",
"Sandbox-and-stop \u2014 the other signals still get their vote."],
"When would you extend a detonation or add a time-box despite a clean report? Give two cases."),
]
for _l in te_lessons: new_lessons.append(_l)
