ua = {"id": "unified-audit", "name": "Unified Audit",
"description": "The source of truth for endpoint operations: reading audit entries, prevalence, retention, and turning logs into decisions.",
"icon": "\U0001f50d", "scenarios": []}
ua_lessons = [
L("unified-audit", "Anatomy of an audit entry",
["Every audit entry: WHO (user/device), WHAT (binary, hash, signer), WHICH RULE (or 'default-deny, no match'), WHEN (timestamp), PREVALENCE (endpoints affected), VERDICT. Six lines \u2014 extract them before theorizing.",
"The rule-that-fired distinguishes inventory misses ('no matching allow') from enforced decisions (named rule). Same block, different investigation: the first fixes the catalog, the second reviews the rule.",
"Prevalence is the triage accelerator: 200 endpoints + signed = systemic (rule error or version churn); 1 endpoint + unsigned + 3 AM = targeted (investigate the box). Read prevalence before details.",
"Signer state is the instant sort: known-vendor-signed (probable false positive), unsigned-first-seen (probable unknown), unknown-signer (stop and verify the signer). Three buckets, seconds to sort."],
"AUDIT ANATOMY\n  who:   k.osei / FIN-04\n  what:  updater.exe | sha256:9f2c | signed Acme Inc\n  rule:  default-deny (no match)\n  when:  08:04 | prevalence: 14 endpoints\n  verdict: DENY",
"Extract the six lines \u2014 who, what, rule, when, prevalence, verdict \u2014 before theorizing. The rule-that-fired names the investigation type, prevalence separates systemic from targeted, and signer state sorts in seconds.",
["Theorizing before extracting \u2014 the six lines first, always.",
"Ignoring prevalence \u2014 it separates systemic from targeted in seconds.",
"Skipping the rule-that-fired \u2014 it names the investigation type."],
"Rewrite three scenario evidence blocks as the six-line anatomy."),
L("unified-audit", "Prevalence: the fleet's voice",
["Prevalence turns one event into fleet intelligence: 'this binary denied on 200 endpoints' is a different universe from 'denied on 1.' The audit aggregates; the operator interprets.",
"High prevalence + signed = policy/systemic issue (version churn, rule error, push regression). The fix is central: one rule change fixes 200 endpoints. Don't troubleshoot 200 machines.",
"Low prevalence + unsigned + odd context = targeted investigation: the box, the user, the delivery. The fix is local: isolate, investigate, hunt laterally for the delivery \u2014 not the binary.",
"Zero prevalence ('first seen ever') is itself a signal: the binary is new to YOUR fleet. New-to-fleet + unsigned = the sandbox queue. New-to-fleet + signed-by-known = probable legitimate new version."],
"PREVALENCE READS\n  200 + signed   -> systemic: fix the rule centrally\n  1 + unsigned + 3AM -> targeted: investigate the box\n  0 (first seen) + unsigned -> sandbox queue\n  0 + signed-known -> probable legit new version",
"Let prevalence direct the response: high-plus-signed gets the central rule fix, low-plus-unsigned gets the box investigation, first-seen gets the sandbox queue. The fleet is talking \u2014 the operator listens first.",
["Troubleshooting 200 machines for a rule error \u2014 fix centrally.",
"Treating first-seen as guilty \u2014 new-to-fleet is a signal, not a verdict.",
"Ignoring prevalence entirely \u2014 the fleet is talking; listen."],
"Classify five invented audit entries by prevalence read alone."),
L("unified-audit", "Retention, integrity, and the auditor",
["Retention: the audit must cover the periods you'll be asked about (quarters, incident windows). Define it, fund the storage, TEST restores \u2014 an audit you can't query is an archive, not a control.",
"Integrity: audit logs must be tamper-evident (ideally tamper-proof): append-only, access-controlled, with admin actions themselves audited. An attacker who can edit the audit can edit history.",
"The auditor's questions: 'show me no unapproved execution in Q3' (the negative: deny coverage + policy completeness) and 'show me every exception's approval' (the positive: the register). Both come from this audit plus the exception records.",
"Self-audit quarterly: ask the auditor's questions of yourself before they do. The gaps you find are remediation; the gaps they find are findings."],
"AUDIT HYGIENE\n  retention: defined, funded, restore-tested\n  integrity:  append-only, admin actions audited\n  auditor:    negative (no unapproved exec) + positive (exceptions)\n  self-audit: quarterly, before they do",
"Design retention around the questions you'll be asked, make the audit tamper-evident, and self-audit quarterly. The auditor's two proofs \u2014 the negative and the positive \u2014 come from this audit plus the exception register.",
["Untested restores \u2014 the archive you can't query.",
"Editable audits \u2014 history the attacker can revise.",
"Annual scramble \u2014 hygiene is continuous, not seasonal."],
"Check your posture: retention period? restore tested? admin actions audited?"),
L("unified-audit", "From audit to action: the ops loop",
["The daily audit loop: SCAN (10-min pattern read: clusters? anomalies? new first-seens?), TRIAGE (systemic \u2192 rule fix; targeted \u2192 investigate; unknown \u2192 sandbox), ACT (ticketed, narrow), VERIFY (did the pattern change tomorrow?).",
"The scan is pattern-first: same binary many endpoints, same endpoint many binaries, new signer first-seen, odd-hour activity. Ten minutes of pattern reading beats an hour of ticket order.",
"Close the loop visibly: yesterday's systemic fix should move today's numbers. If the pattern persists after the fix, the fix was wrong \u2014 the audit tells you, if you look. The loop is the learning.",
"Escalation from the audit: correlated signals (ringfence deny + egress deny, same host, same hour) escalate to incident WITHOUT waiting for a ticket. The audit is a detection source, not just a record."],
"DAILY AUDIT LOOP\n  scan:    10-min pattern read (clusters, anomalies, first-seens)\n  triage:  systemic->rule | targeted->investigate | unknown->sandbox\n  act:     ticketed, narrow\n  verify:  did the pattern move tomorrow?\n  escalate: correlated signals -> incident, no ticket needed",
"Run the daily loop: ten-minute pattern scan, triage by pattern type, ticketed narrow action, and verify tomorrow that the pattern moved. Correlated signals escalate to incident directly \u2014 the audit is a detection source.",
["Ticket-order reading \u2014 patterns first, tickets second.",
"Fix without verify-tomorrow \u2014 the loop must close.",
"Waiting for tickets on correlated signals \u2014 the audit escalates directly."],
"Run the 10-minute scan on an invented audit day: list the patterns you'd flag."),
]
for _l in ua_lessons: new_lessons.append(_l)
